---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { getState, href } from '../../lib/data';
import { breadcrumbNode, pageGraph, webPageNode } from '../../lib/schema';

const title = 'Texas HOA rules for an RV or boat | RV Storage Prices';
const description =
  'Texas Property Code section 209.015 mentions a recreational vehicle only inside an adjacent-lot definition, and only if the dedicatory instrument already permits it.';
const path = '/rules/texas/';
const checked = '2026-11-19';
const texas = getState('texas');
const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { label: 'HOA rules', path: '/rules/' },
  { label: 'Texas', path },
];
const schema = pageGraph([
  webPageNode({ title, description, path, dateModified: checked }),
  breadcrumbNode(crumbs),
]);
---

<Base title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <h1>Texas HOA rules for an RV or boat</h1>
    <p>
      This page quotes the Texas Property Code. It is not legal advice, not a ruling on your dedicatory
      instrument, and not a substitute for the city or county code.
    </p>
    <p>
      Checked <time datetime={checked}>November 19, 2026</time> on the
      <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/PR/htm/PR.209.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer">Texas Constitution and Statutes copy of Property Code Chapter 209</a>.
      The only use of “recreational vehicle” in that chapter file is inside section 209.015. The
      <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/PR/htm/PR.202.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer">Chapter 202 file</a>
      retrieved the same day did not contain those words. Section 202.010 in that file is “Regulation of
      solar energy devices,” so this page does not cite it as a vehicle rule. No section was found that
      voids an association ban on an RV or a boat the way Florida’s section 720.3045 does. No section
      naming a boat was found in either file.
    </p>
    <h2>The adjacent-lot definition</h2>
    <p>
      Section 209.015 is titled “Regulation of land use: residential purpose.” The history line on the
      file says it was added by Acts 2013, 83rd Leg., R.S., Ch. 219 (H.B. 35), Sec. 1, effective June
      14, 2013, and amended by Acts 2021, 87th Leg., R.S., Ch. 951 (S.B. 1588), Sec. 19, effective
      September 1, 2021. The quote below is the text posted on the date above. This page does not say
      which words the 2021 act changed.
    </p>
    <p>Section 209.015(a)(2) says “residential purpose” with respect to the use of a lot:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        (B) includes the location on the lot of a garage, sidewalk, driveway, parking area, children's
        swing or playscape, fence, septic system, swimming pool, utility line, or water well and, if
        otherwise specifically permitted by the dedicatory instrument, the parking or storage of a
        recreational vehicle.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Subsection (b) says:</p>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        Except as provided by this section, a property owners' association may not adopt or enforce a
        provision in a dedicatory instrument that prohibits or restricts the owner of a lot on which a
        residence is located from using for residential purposes an adjacent lot owned by the property
        owner.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <h2>What that section does not decide</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The recreational-vehicle words sit inside a definition, and only “if otherwise specifically
        permitted by the dedicatory instrument.” The section does not require the instrument to permit
        an RV.
      </li>
      <li>
        Subsection (b) is about using an adjacent lot for a residential purpose. It is not a rule that
        an RV may be parked on the lot where the residence already stands.
      </li>
      <li>
        Subsection (c) still requires association or architectural-review approval, based on criteria in
        the dedicatory instruments, before an owner places an improvement for that residential purpose
        on the adjacent lot. This page does not apply that approval sentence to a particular RV.
      </li>
      <li>
        The section does not mention a boat. A city or county rule is outside this quote.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Off-site storage is a different question</h2>
    <p>
      A facility listing in this directory is a storage lot, not a statement that an association allows
      the vehicle at the house. {texas.cities.length} Texas city pages list those lots.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        texas.cities.map((city) => (
          <li>
            <a href={href(`/cities/${city.id}/`)}>
              {city.city}, {city.state_code}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </article>
</Base>
